<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alawites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nusayris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alawites are a religious community that emerged from Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 9th and 10th centuries, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali and associated with the teacher Ibn Nusayr, hence the older name Nusayris. Concentrated in the coastal mountains of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and around Antakya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, they were long marginalised under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Under the French Mandate they had their own Alawite State, and from 1970 the Alawite Assad family ruled Syria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alawites.jpg" alt="Alawites" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Hurley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celebrating_Alawites.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Alawites trace their faith to Muhammad ibn Nusayr, an Iraqi teacher of the ninth century who was a disciple of the Twelver imams Ali al-Hadi and Hasan al-Askari. After al-Askari died, a dispute over his succession arose in the Shia community, and Ibn Nusayr claimed to be the Bab (Door) and Ism (Name) of the dead imam and to have received his secret teachings. His teaching appears to be one of several early mystical ghulat movements.</p><p>The community was organised under Hamdanid rule in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> (947–1008) by al-Khaṣībī, a follower of Ibn Nusayr who died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> about 969. His grandson and pupil Abu Sa'id Maymun al-Tabarani moved in 1032 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a>, then held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and died in 1034. The historian Stefan Winter calls him the last definitive scholar of Alawism, who founded its calendar and gave its teachings their final form.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Alawites have faced periods of subjugation or persecution under Muslim empires including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Until the 1920s the community usually called itself Nusayri, after Ibn Nusayr. Intellectuals within it then promoted the name Alawi, to stress a link with mainstream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and especially Shia Islam, and a name the French authorities also used.</p><p>The French Mandate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, established in 1920, was a turning point. The French recruited many minority members into their armed forces and created areas reserved for minorities, among them the Alawite State. That state was later dismantled, but Alawites kept a prominent place in the Syrian army and later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a> took power in the 1970 coup, the state promoted a national ideology among Alawites to replace their traditional identity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Alawite doctrine is secretive: some teachings are revealed only to a chosen few, and the community has practised taqiya, the concealment of belief, to avoid persecution. Its central belief is a divine Trinity of Ma'na (meaning), Ism (Name) and Bab (Door), which Alawites hold to have been incarnated seven times in human form. The seventh incarnation consists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, Muhammad and Salman al-Farisi. Alawites also believe in reincarnation, and the faith has been described as syncretic, drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnosticism">Gnosticism</a>, neo-Platonism and Christian practices such as a Mass with bread and wine.</p><p>Alawites have traditionally lived in the coastal mountains of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tartus</a> the main cities, and on the plains around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>. They make up about 9 to 12 percent of Syria's population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alawites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Mediterranean coast and the mountains of the west across the fertile valleys of the Euphrates, Orontes and Khabur to the eastern desert, with Damascus and Aleppo among the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world and a people of Sunni Arabs, <b>Alawites</b>, Druze, Christians, Kurds and Armenians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1920 France established the Territory of the <b>Alawites</b>, covering the coastal mountains and the port of Latakia, where the Alawites, a heterodox Shia community long marginalised under the Ottomans, were the majority in the countryside.</blockquote><a href="/state/alawite-state">Alawite State</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Ankara of 1921 France agreed that the district of Alexandretta, which included Antioch and a large Turkish-speaking population alongside Arabs, <b>Alawites</b> and Armenians, should have a special administrative regime within French-ruled Syria.</blockquote><a href="/state/sanjak-of-alexandretta">Sanjak of Alexandretta</a></li></ul></section>
